  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Pooling Layers** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef dl_pooling_layers(x: list, pool_size: int = 2, mode: str = 'max') -> list:\n    arr = np.array(x, dtype=float)\n    oh, ow = arr.shape[0] // pool_size, arr.shape[1] // pool_size\n    out = np.zeros((oh, ow))\n    for i in range(oh):\n        for j in range(ow):\n            p = arr[i*pool_size:(i+1)*pool_size, j*pool_size:(j+1)*pool_size]\n            out[i, j] = np.max(p) if mode == 'max' else np.mean(p)\n    return np.round(out, 4).tolist()\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
